What this solution actually does: SwfINMQ is a timer-driven Windows Service that ingests inbound SWIFT messages from an IBM MQ queue into the TOMI (InterfacesT24) database. Each tick opens a SimpleInjector scope, marks the service as running, reloads 25 system parameters (per-weekday operating hours, MQ host/channel/queue/credential/TLS settings, the operations mailbox), and decides from the database business date plus the local clock whether the service is inside its operating window. Inside the window it connects to the queue manager over TLS, drains the queue with a 5-second wait Get, and passes each message to usp_SWF_INSERT_MQ_MSG. That procedure takes the first 17 characters as the ICN, creates the work-plan master row (READ) and the work row that stores the whole message when they do not already exist, and returns the ICN. An unexpected exception while reading or storing a message is logged and emailed (with the message text) to the operations mailbox and the cycle ends without rethrowing; failures outside the read loop (parameter loading, lifecycle updates, configuration) raise a CRITICO alert instead. The consumers of TB_SRV_SWF_PLAN_TRABAJO_MAESTRO / TB_SRV_SWF_TRABAJO are outside this repository, so downstream processing is inferred from the table names only.
