5. Architecture-Fit Note
The workload is a continuous-intake, poll-based consumer: a timer-driven Windows Service that reconnects to the queue manager on every tick, drains the queue, and writes each message to a work table that other SWIFT-incoming components consume. It is already inherently message-driven, so the lowest-risk path is to modernize in place after correcting the findings, following the sequence in _ORCHESTRATION.md section 6: first remove the loss and exposure risks (MQ-FR-8, MQ-FR-9, MQ-FR-12, MQ-FR-10, MQ-FR-11) and the schedule/lifecycle defects (MQ-FR-1 to MQ-FR-5); then remove the duplication, restore the MQ seam, and add real tests (MQ-FR-6, MQ-FR-13, MQ-FR-18); and only then evaluate hosting modernization. The target shape is the standard reliable-consumer pattern (transactional get, idempotent persist, commit, backout/dead-letter queue) rather than a new platform. A persistent MQ connection or an asynchronous message listener would replace the connect/poll/disconnect cycle (about 8,640 cycles a day at the 10-second default interval); a .NET 8 Worker Service would align hosting with current Microsoft guidance. Those options depend on facts this audit does not have: queue volume and peak rate, intake-latency SLA, whether the operating window is a business rule or only a legacy throttle (messages wait safely in MQ outside it), whether weekend or overnight intake is required, hosting constraints for the 32-bit managed MQ client, and budget. A microservices rewrite is not justified by the current evidence.
